WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.901
-Hello and welcome.

00:00:01.901 --> 00:00:03.769
Thank you so much
for coming on our show.

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:05.037
-Thank you for having me.

00:00:05.037 --> 00:00:07.073
It's a delight.
I'm excited to be here.

00:00:07.073 --> 00:00:08.274
-I'm happy you're here.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:11.077
Congrats on the book.
It's fantastic.

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:14.914
I want to talk about
your historic run,

00:00:14.914 --> 00:00:19.052
and Obama got behind you,
Oprah got behind you.

00:00:19.052 --> 00:00:20.353
Do you remember getting
the phone call

00:00:20.353 --> 00:00:22.722
that Oprah wants to support?

00:00:22.722 --> 00:00:24.190
-Oh, yeah, so, she called,

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.292
and we had to pull off
on the side of the highway

00:00:26.292 --> 00:00:28.661
because we were heading into
one of those parts of Georgia

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:30.963
that did not have cell service.

00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:34.000
And I sort of scared the driver
like, "We got to pull over now!"

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:36.669
-"Oprah is on the phone!
Do whatever you have to do!"

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:38.471
-Seriously.

00:00:38.471 --> 00:00:40.840
We, like, barely avoided
an aardvark or something.

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:44.110
-[ Laughs ]
-It was dynamic.

00:00:44.110 --> 00:00:47.547
-Your book, "Our Time Is Now,"

00:00:47.547 --> 00:00:53.720
you wanted it to come out now,
as soon as possible because why?

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:55.221
Tell people.

00:00:55.221 --> 00:00:57.756
-One is that we're
in the midst of primaries

00:00:57.756 --> 00:01:00.426
and we often think
that voter suppression

00:01:00.426 --> 00:01:02.862
only exists
in the general election,

00:01:02.862 --> 00:01:04.897
that it's Democrats
versus Republicans.

00:01:04.897 --> 00:01:07.233
I wanted people to really
understand that suppression

00:01:07.233 --> 00:01:09.769
is a system and that system
exists year-round,

00:01:09.769 --> 00:01:13.539
whether we're picking our side
or they're picking their side.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:16.275
And if you don't understand
that it's always present,

00:01:16.275 --> 00:01:19.045
then you are going to ignore it
or think that it's just about

00:01:19.045 --> 00:01:20.747
politicians being mad
at each other.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:22.815
So I wanted to use the book
and the timing

00:01:22.815 --> 00:01:24.250
to give people
time to absorb it,

00:01:24.250 --> 00:01:26.386
to understand it,
and to know what to look for

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:29.189
so we can fight for
fair elections in November.

00:01:29.189 --> 00:01:31.223
-The beginning of the book,
the introduction,

00:01:31.223 --> 00:01:32.525
you tell this great story

00:01:32.525 --> 00:01:34.927
about the first time
your grandma voted.

00:01:34.927 --> 00:01:39.532
-So, my grandmother -- we call
her -- her name is Wilter.

00:01:39.532 --> 00:01:40.833
They call her Bill.

00:01:40.833 --> 00:01:43.603
I was very confused as a child
for a very long time

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:46.005
because my grandfather,
his name was Walter

00:01:46.005 --> 00:01:48.607
and they called him Jim,
so it was a lot of names

00:01:48.607 --> 00:01:51.043
that did not make sense.

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.446
So, you know,
during my campaign,

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:57.049
I was home for a visit and I was
talking to my grandmother.

00:01:57.049 --> 00:02:00.419
She was watching what was
happening here in Georgia

00:02:00.419 --> 00:02:03.522
as more and more about the voter
suppression was coming out.

00:02:03.522 --> 00:02:06.692
And she decided to tell me about
the first time she voted.

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:07.960
She didn't want to.

00:02:07.960 --> 00:02:12.297
She had watched her children
be arrested and attacked

00:02:12.297 --> 00:02:14.066
during the civil rights
movement.

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:16.902
Her husband had served in
two wars on behalf of

00:02:16.902 --> 00:02:18.905
the United States
and was never allowed to vote.

00:02:18.905 --> 00:02:21.107
She had been, you know,
assailed herself

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:25.177
and she just didn't trust that
the right to vote was real.

00:02:25.177 --> 00:02:27.947
My grandfather kept urging her
from the front room

00:02:27.947 --> 00:02:30.082
to come and vote
because that was during the time

00:02:30.082 --> 00:02:32.184
where people used to dress up
to go and vote.

00:02:32.184 --> 00:02:35.088
And it was the first time
black people in Mississippi

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.724
would have had a chance to vote
in a presidential election

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.525
in modern times.

00:02:39.525 --> 00:02:43.096
And she just didn't want to go,
and when she told me the story,

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:44.597
she wanted me to understand

00:02:44.597 --> 00:02:48.701
it wasn't that she was --
it wasn't just the fear.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.937
Not just fear of the guns
and the hoses and the dogs.

00:02:51.937 --> 00:02:53.839
It was fear of the power.

00:02:53.839 --> 00:02:56.008
For something to be denied
for so long,

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:58.144
it was hard to believe
it was real.

00:02:58.144 --> 00:03:00.713
And where my grandfather
was excited about it,

00:03:00.713 --> 00:03:03.582
she was just filled with
trepidation and shame

00:03:03.582 --> 00:03:05.651
that she didn't want to risk it

00:03:05.651 --> 00:03:09.855
just in case this time
it was also a lie.

00:03:09.855 --> 00:03:11.624
-It's unbelievable
when you hear those stories

00:03:11.624 --> 00:03:14.694
and it's, you know,
here we are today,

00:03:14.694 --> 00:03:17.196
and there's voter suppression
still today.

00:03:17.196 --> 00:03:20.132
I mean, we saw this happen
in Georgia.

00:03:20.132 --> 00:03:21.401
It was insane.

00:03:21.401 --> 00:03:24.203
And people are waiting outside
during a pandemic.

00:03:24.203 --> 00:03:27.373
And you go,
"What the heck is going on?"

00:03:27.373 --> 00:03:30.042
How do we stop this?

00:03:30.042 --> 00:03:31.944
-Well, the first thing
is that if you want to

00:03:31.944 --> 00:03:34.179
dismantle a system,
you got to understand it.

00:03:34.179 --> 00:03:36.148
-Yeah.
-And you know what

00:03:36.148 --> 00:03:37.616
my grandmother wanted me
to understand,

00:03:37.616 --> 00:03:40.520
what I want folks to
understand is that the age of,

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.822
you know, guns
and dogs and hoses

00:03:42.822 --> 00:03:45.190
being the barrier to voting
is gone.

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:49.595
Today it's labyrinthine
administrative rules.

00:03:49.595 --> 00:03:52.898
It's having to know based on
the day you move to a new state

00:03:52.898 --> 00:03:55.568
how many days you have
until you can get registered.

00:03:55.568 --> 00:03:58.070
It's being able to register
to vote

00:03:58.070 --> 00:04:00.406
and not have your application
thrown out

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.575
because someone didn't know
how to spell your last name

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:04.243
and wouldn't ask.

00:04:04.243 --> 00:04:06.813
But it's also about having
a polling place shut down,

00:04:06.813 --> 00:04:08.714
which is part of what happened
in Georgia.

00:04:08.714 --> 00:04:13.352
We had one polling location
that had to serve 16,000 people

00:04:13.352 --> 00:04:16.255
in a single day
with the same number of machines

00:04:16.255 --> 00:04:19.392
they used to have when they were
serving only a few thousand.

00:04:19.392 --> 00:04:22.228
And so it's understanding
that it's complex now,

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:24.930
that it's designed
to look like user error.

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:28.634
It's designed to look like
just bureaucracy.

00:04:28.634 --> 00:04:30.435
And it's designed
to make you doubt

00:04:30.435 --> 00:04:32.805
that you didn't
make the mistake.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:35.107
And that's what's so insidious
about it.

00:04:35.107 --> 00:04:37.676
We no longer treat
voter suppression

00:04:37.676 --> 00:04:39.545
as the responsibility

00:04:39.545 --> 00:04:41.180
of those who are
in charge of elections.

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:44.016
We blame each other
instead of blaming the system

00:04:44.016 --> 00:04:45.885
and blaming the people
responsible

00:04:45.885 --> 00:04:47.854
for making the system so hard.

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:49.322
-You also talk in the book about

00:04:49.322 --> 00:04:52.057
how important
the census is as well.

00:04:52.057 --> 00:04:54.294
-So the census is that --
you know, it seems like

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:57.296
this annoying, intrusive,
statistical thing

00:04:57.296 --> 00:04:59.065
that happens every 10 years.
-Yeah.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:02.001
-Most of us respond just because
we believe the red letters

00:05:02.001 --> 00:05:04.170
saying this is a federal crime
if you don't reply.

00:05:04.170 --> 00:05:08.140
But too often, the people
who most need to be seen

00:05:08.140 --> 00:05:09.509
are afraid of it.

00:05:09.509 --> 00:05:12.244
And unfortunately,
in this administration,

00:05:12.244 --> 00:05:14.747
Donald Trump
has been very intentional

00:05:14.747 --> 00:05:16.783
about trying to
scare people out of

00:05:16.783 --> 00:05:19.018
completing the census
or underfunding it.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:23.990
And it's based on an analysis
that they got from a gentleman

00:05:23.990 --> 00:05:27.126
named Thomas Hofeller, who said
that if you can get people

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.694
not to participate in
the census,

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:30.463
especially communities of color,

00:05:30.463 --> 00:05:34.800
the nation would look
more Republican and white.

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:36.902
And that's just not who we are.

00:05:36.902 --> 00:05:39.271
Those communities
that are being overlooked now,

00:05:39.271 --> 00:05:40.706
that are suffering now,

00:05:40.706 --> 00:05:43.375
it's because they were
discounted 10 years ago.

00:05:43.375 --> 00:05:45.445
But it's also political power.

00:05:45.445 --> 00:05:48.281
The districts
that get drawn for elections

00:05:48.281 --> 00:05:50.249
are drawn based on
these numbers.

00:05:50.249 --> 00:05:54.453
And if you aren't counted, you
are counted out for 10 years.

00:05:54.453 --> 00:05:56.254
-10 years.
-And 10 years.

00:05:56.254 --> 00:05:59.492
And that's a long time to go,
to be ignored by those

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:02.328
who are responsible for
making choices on your behalf.

00:06:02.328 --> 00:06:04.063
-Stacey, you're on
the short list

00:06:04.063 --> 00:06:08.701
of a possible VP running mate
for Joe Biden.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:11.170
Obviously, you can't speak
much about that,

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:16.475
but if that does happen,
who is your first call?

00:06:16.475 --> 00:06:18.844
-Oh, my mom and dad.

00:06:18.844 --> 00:06:20.445
-Really?

00:06:20.445 --> 00:06:23.716
I mean, they got to be so proud
with everything you're doing.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.951
-I said mom first because she's
the most likely to answer

00:06:25.951 --> 00:06:27.052
the phone when I call,

00:06:27.052 --> 00:06:28.287
but she'll call Daddy
over to listen.

00:06:28.287 --> 00:06:31.890
-Oh, yeah.
My mom was like that too, yeah.

00:06:31.890 --> 00:06:34.560
My dad wouldn't even go
near the phone.

00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.462
That was her thing,
and she would get on

00:06:36.462 --> 00:06:37.730
and she would just
talk and talk.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:40.899
I could put the phone down,
walk down the block,

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:43.669
come back, and we're still
mid-conversation.

00:06:43.669 --> 00:06:46.672
But I love that
that would be your first call.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:48.374
Thank you so much
for coming on our show.

00:06:48.374 --> 00:06:49.976
I really appreciate it.

00:06:49.976 --> 00:06:52.678
And I hope to talk to you
in the future.

00:06:52.678 --> 00:06:55.748
-Thank you so much.
I truly am honored to be here.

